  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 220 PM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A dying cold front is approaching northern New \nYork and Vermont from Canada this afternoon, producing some \nshowers out ahead of it. The forcing will decrease as it moves \neast through northern New York, but enough instability is out \nthere to allow the showers to continue into Vermont. A couple \nembedded thunderstorms are possible as well. While sufficient \ndeep layer shear will be in place for potentially strong storms,\nlow instability, some dry air aloft, a lack of well-defined \nsurface boundary, and pre-existing clouds/showers will be \nworking against it. Therefore, an isolated strong to severe \nstorm is not likely this afternoon, but cannot be completely \nruled out. Any storms will fall apart as they try to push \nsoutheast this evening and southern areas should remain mostly \ndry. Chances of lingering showers overnight are around 10-40%, \nmainly near the international border. High pressure will build \ndown from Canada to end the week and start the weekend, bringing\ncooler temperatures and lower humidity. Lows tonight will be \nseasonable in the 50s, then cooling late week into the 40s and \n50s. Highs through Saturday will be seasonable, with \ntemperatures rising into the 70s and low 80s each afternoon. \nNext chance of precipitation arrives later Saturday afternoon \nonward.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A quasi-double barrel pattern will develop by \nlate Saturday with competing pieces of energy, one over central-\neastern Ontario, and a coastal low in southern New England. \nModels depict a deepening trough over the Great Lakes by \nSaturday with a coastal low riding along the coast of southern \nNew England. The deepening of the Great Lakes trough will help \namplify our flow pattern aloft Saturday night into Sunday, \nlikely accelerating the coastal low northeastward. A few showers\nwill be possible on the periphery of the coastal low Saturday \nnight in southeastern Vermont, though majority of Vermont will \nstay dry Saturday and Saturday night. In northern New York, the \nGreat Lakes trough will lead to increasing shower chances late \nSaturday into Saturday night across the St. Lawrence Valley and \nnorthwestern Adirondacks. Questions linger on how much moisture \nwill be sandwiched between the two systems closer to Sunday \nmorning, as it pertains to precipitation chances in Vermont. \nCurrently there is a 35-50% chance of rain Sunday morning for \nmuch of Vermont, though these values may increase or decrease \nwith how the trough amplification occurs. A more delayed \namplification could keep the coastal system from racing east \nwith a moisture interaction in the area between the system \nSunday morning. A faster amplification will likely lead to less \nsystem interactions and perhaps a dry Sunday morning. \nRegardless, as the Great Lakes trough translates east Sunday, \neveryone will see periods of rain, and perhaps some \nthunderstorms should enough clearing take place to allow \ninstability to grow. Temperatures will start off on the warmer \nthan average side in the upper 70s to low 80s, though with the \napproach of clouds and showers, temperatures by Sunday will \nbegin a gradual decrease back to seasonable levels.\n\nThe main piece of energy departs late Sunday, though cyclonic \nflow about a trailing upper low over eastern Ontario will keep \nsome shower chances around. Lingering showers will be focused \nover the higher terrain Monday and Tuesday afternoon, mainly \ndiurnally driven. Sky cover Monday and Tuesday will favor more \nclouds then sun as a result of the scattered shower chances. \nTemperatures will remain on the seasonable to potentially \nslightly below seasonable levels Monday and Tuesday with highs \nin the low to mid 70s. Temperatures will rebound by mid week \nwith subtle ridging building over eastern New England.",
